/**
 * @mainpage Comfort Select SDK Backend - High-Level Architecture
 *
 * Welcome to the official C++ developer documentation for the **Comfort Select SDK Backend**.
 * This core static library provides a professional, thread-safe, and dependency-injected framework to communicate
 * with bio-impedance scales, manage user profiles, compute body composition, and persist records.
 *
 * For the frontend user interface component, see the dedicated documentation page:
 * - @ref gui_doc
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section arch_overview 1. Architectural Design & Core Backend Components
 *
 * The `libscale` library strictly adheres to **SOLID Principles**, **RAII Memory Management**, and the **Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)**.
 * The core components are decoupled via abstract interfaces to maximize testability and reusability:
 *
 * @subsection arch_manager A. Service Orchestrator (HealthManager)
 * The central service layer coordinating database access, hardware communication, metrics calculation,
 * and asynchronous background synchronization via a custom multithreaded worker.
 * - See: libscale::HealthManager
 *
 * @subsection arch_hardware B. Hardware Interface (IScaleDevice)
 * Abstract interface encapsulating USB physical connection protocol logic. 
 * The concrete implementation `UsbScaleDevice` uses `libusb` and features an event-driven hotplug monitoring thread.
 * - See: libscale::IScaleDevice, libscale::UsbScaleDevice
 *
 * @subsection arch_calculator C. Calculation Engine (IBioImpedanceCalculator)
 * Processes raw scale payloads (impedance at 5 kHz and 50 kHz, raw weight) and computes high-level metrics
 * (BMI, body fat, body water, muscle mass, daily kilocalorie needs) based on formula algorithms.
 * - See: libscale::IBioImpedanceCalculator, libscale::BioImpedanceCalculatorV1
 *
 * @subsection arch_database D. Data Persistence (IDatabaseManager)
 * Abstract boundary managing health profile storage. Concrete subclass `SqliteDatabaseManager` leverages
 * `sqlite_orm` with specialized converters mapping custom strong physical units safely to databases.
 * - See: libscale::IDatabaseManager, libscale::SqliteDatabaseManager, sqlite_orm
 *
 * ---
 *
 * @section quickstart 2. Quick Start Example
 *
 * The following C++ example demonstrates initializing the backend services, registering a user profile,
 * and reading stored measurements:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <memory>
 * #include <iostream>
 * #include "libscale/database_impl.hpp"
 * #include "libscale/device.hpp"
 * #include "libscale/manager.hpp"
 *
 * int main() {
 *     // 1. Instantiate SQLite storage layer
 *     auto database = std::make_unique<libscale::SqliteDatabaseManager>();
 *     if (!database->initialize("libscale_health.db")) {
 *         std::cerr << "Failed to initialize database!" << std::endl;
 *         return 1;
 *     }
 *
 *     // 2. Instantiate USB hardware communication layer
 *     auto device = std::make_unique<libscale::UsbScaleDevice>();
 *
 *     // 3. Coordinate under HealthManager orchestrator (injecting dependencies)
 *     libscale::HealthManager manager(*database, *device);
 *     
 *     // 4. Fetch and print user names
 *     auto users = manager.get_users();
 *     for (const auto& user : users) {
 *         std::cout << "User Slot [" << user.slot_id << "]: " << user.name << std::endl;
 *     }
 *
 *     return 0;
 * }
 * @endcode
 */
